-### Distance Distortion
-A method to handle any Lipschitz transformation of the KRS geometry by sacrificing some performance [S1, unofficial]. While standard KRS has a Lipschitz constant of unity, this variant allows for non-uniform scaling or twisting distortions (e.g., to avoid conspicuous symmetries in tree-like structures) by applying the transformation to the distance evaluation, though it requires careful handling to maintain the safety of the sphere tracing step size [S1, unofficial].
+A technique to combine multiple KRSs to produce natural disorder and hide symmetry by taking the union of their geometries [S1]. It maintains the free distance along the ray for all KRS components, always choosing the minimal one, and advances the ray with the radius of the unbounding sphere of the respective component [S1]. The search ends when the step size for the minimal component meets the error threshold [S1]. Performance does not depend on the number of KRS components, but rather on the number of sphere tracing steps required, with a slight overhead for maintaining a small sorted index of free distances [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The distance evaluation is $O(n)$ where $n$ is the number of reflection levels, independent of recursion depth [S1, unofficial]. Sphere tracing performance depends on the number of steps required to converge, which is influenced by the threshold and geometry complexity [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires only a few registers of read-write memory, independent of the number of levels, as the algorithm is iterative and non-recursive [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Enables real-time rendering on parallel hardware (GPUs) due to the fixed operation count and lack of divergent control flow associated with recursion [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Continuous level-of-detail, automatic minification filtering, no visible symmetry artifacts when combined with multiple KRSs or distortions, efficient GPU implementation [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Standard KRS is limited to reflection-multiplied instances of the same, unscaled kernel geometry; hierarchies and non-symmetric parts require additional kernels or distortions [S1, unofficial]. Higher thresholds reduce detail at a distance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The distance evaluation requires a fixed number of operations proportional to the number of levels $n$, independent of recursion depth [S1]. Sphere tracing performance depends on the number of steps required, not the number of KRS components in the union case [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires only a few registers of read-write memory, independent of the number of levels [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables real-time rendering on parallel hardware (GPUs) due to non-recursive, fixed-operation-count algorithm [S1]. Provides continuous level-of-detail and minification filtering automatically [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Single KRS constructions exhibit conspicuous symmetries, requiring additional techniques (multiple KRSs, non-symmetric kernels, distortion) to appear natural [S1]. Higher thresholds reduce sub-pixel detail [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering of complex, fractal-like procedural geometry is required on GPUs, and symmetry can be mitigated.
+*   **When not to use:** When exact geometric precision is required at very small scales without aliasing, as higher thresholds may cause merging of distant geometries [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Kernel-Reflection Sequences were proposed as a procedural modeling approach inspired by iterated function systems (IFS) [S1, unofficial]. The method was introduced to allow for real-time rendering without concessions in visible detail, specifically addressing the limitations of recursive IFS on parallel hardware [S1, unofficial]. It extends sphere tracing to conformally transformed geometries and includes techniques for GPU load balancing [S1, unofficial]. The approach has been used to realize various natural phenomena in uncompromising detail and extents, such as forests and organic structures, without obvious clues of symmetry [S1, unofficial].
+Kernel-Reflection Sequences were proposed in a paper by the authors of [S1] to allow for real-time rendering of procedural models without concessions in visible detail. The approach is inspired by iterated function systems (IFS) but avoids their recursive nature [S1]. It allows evaluation of a global distance function without recursion, enabling real-time sphere tracing on parallel hardware [S1]. The method also extends sphere tracing to conformally transformed geometries and includes a GPU load balancing scheme [S1].
 
